'''Little Hachijo''' ({{lang-bl|Piqueño Hachijo}}), more commonly known as {{Nihongo|'''Hachijoko'''|八丈小|Small Hachijo}}, is an [[w:Ethnic enclave|ethnic enclave]] in [[Castejón]]'s [[Limón]] [[Boroughs of Castejón|borough]] and the largest [[Hachijo people|Hachijō]] [[w:ethnic enclave|enclave]] outside [[w:Asia|Asia]]. It is also one of the largest of the [[Ethnic enclaves in Balisca|over twenty ethnic enclaves within Castejón]].

Similar to nearby [[Okatown]], it's residents resisted {{W|gentrification}}, and remained largely untouched by the 1980s building boom. As a result, many of the houses and shops in the area are small and reflect the character of "pre-boom" Castejón. Due to its aging retail district, narrow streets, and location on a major commuter route, the station area has become a center for small restaurants and "Live Houses" which offer live music. 

There are more bicycles per capita in Hachijoko than any other place in Balisca. It is also known for having a young population and as a center for {{W|urban culture|urban}} {{W|subculture|underground culture}} including multiple used record and clothing shops. It is one of the most walkable areas in Castejón, and the entire neighborhood has been a {{W|pedestrian zone|car-free pedestrian zone}} since ordinance banning automobiles passed in 1996. 

